-- title:
--   Resolvents of a nonexpansive map
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let $K$ be closed and convex, $T:K\to K$ nonexpansive, and $0<t\le1$.
--
--   1. **Affine step.** $S_{a}(v)=t\,a+(1-t)\,T(v)$, a contraction of constant $1-t$ on $K$.
--   2. **Resolvent at weight $t$.** The unique fixed point $J_t(a)$ of $S_a$, given by the Banach contraction principle.
--   3. **Resolvents.** For $n\ge1$, $R_n=J_{1/n}$, so
--   $$
--   R_n(a)=\tfrac1na+\bigl(1-\tfrac1n\bigr)T(R_n(a)).
--   $$
--   Each $R_n$ is nonexpansive. If $\operatorname{diam}K\le1$, then $\|R_n(a)-T(R_n(a))\|\le1/n$.
